            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance news is that NASA/JPL reported on Aug. 5, 2026, that the rover captured a first-of-its-kind view from Mars: Earth disappearing behind the Martian moon Phobos. The sequence was taken by Perseverance’s Mastcam-Z on July 2, 2026 — sol 1,907 of the mission — from the rim of Jezero Crater. NASA described it as the first time an observation has been captured from the surface of another planet showing Earth being occulted by another object【5:0†source】.\n\nAnother recent milestone: Perseverance has now driven the equivalent of a marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles, or 42.195 km. It reached that distance on sol 1,890, after about five years and four months of driving, much faster than Opportunity took to reach the same mark. A Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter HiRISE image also spotted the rover and its tracks from orbit shortly before the milestone【5:3†source】.\n\nIn terms of ongoing science, NASA’s rover updates indicate Perseverance has been exploring beyond Jezero Crater’s rim, studying crater-rim geology, wind-shaped features like megaripples, diverse boulders, and unusual rocks including a possible meteorite candidate【5:1†source】.",
                "annotations": [
